WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
A true story.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.400
I left small-farmer roots
for Budapest, then London,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.700
searching for a more human way
to live and meet.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.700
Even with a multinational job,
Budapest meant workers' hostels

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.100
and unstable rooms,
until my parents helped me

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.450
secure a home.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.650
In London, I found software work
within two weeks.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:26.000
My English barely reached
the lunch table.

00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:34.600
Museums, exhibitions, clubs and dates
across cultures

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.950
made London feel inexhaustible.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:40.250
Home was a shared Hungarian flat:

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.700
up to ten different lives,
one bath, one toilet

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.450
and one shared room.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.900
Some felt happier there
and no longer wanted to go home.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.100
The cheap room made travel possible,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:58.000
but private London housing would erase
my savings. Budapest would not.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:05.400
Across nearly fifty countries,
I discovered places, and myself,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.400
through local people.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.250
Then the world closed.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.650
But before it did,
I had gone out to earn money.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.550
At thirty-five,
I came home to find a partner.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.800
For almost two years, the same photograph
brought no Tinder match.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.650
On Tinder, thirty-five had become
a threshold in who could even see me.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.050
At thirty-seven, I entered thirty-three.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.150
The silence broke.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.474
Even women older than the displayed age
began matching.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.350
Beginner dance floors were full.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:39.050
Advanced rooms thinned out,
as beginnings kept losing their next step.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.850
Sometimes a conversation opened,
then a friendly partner arrived

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.300
and the missing context became clear.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.350
In cafés, basement music
and beautiful tables

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.100
turned first dates into performances.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:58.000
The problem was not people.
It was how we were meeting.

00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:07.100
On a screen, age, appearance and history
arrived before a voice.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.850
The number could decide
whether the table existed.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:13.850
I wanted private, editable intention
and mutual context,

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.000
not a public verdict.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.450
Reciprocal choices could bring
six strangers together.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.000
Each person would still decide:
accept, talk, meet,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.321
introduce themselves,
and make a first plan.

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:38.250
The event would coordinate people,
transport, place and supplies.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.700
After meeting, private experience
could reshape later invitations;

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.100
an open place could be offered again.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.250
Financial independence
gave my 2016 idea time.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.900
AI accelerated one shared core,
shaped and tested by local communities.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.250
MyScoutee.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.416
Test it with me
in real communities.
